## 🔍 Edge Case Analysis

### High-Risk Areas Identified

**1. Security masking reliability (🔴 Critical)**
The masking system relies on pattern detection. Obfuscated credentials (Base64-encoded API keys, hex-encoded passwords, split credentials across lines) may bypass detection. If masking fails silently, PII leaks into output that could be committed to GitHub or shared with the wider team.

**2. Incomplete generation detection (🔴 Critical)**
The skill must detect its own token limit cutoff and stop cleanly after the last complete TC. If the model truncates output exactly mid-Expected-Result, the cleanup logic may not trigger. This scenario is hard to test deterministically and may require repeated runs.

**3. Version rollback across sessions (🔴 Critical)**
The spec promises rollback to previous versions ("Roll back to v1.0"), but Claude Projects have no native persistent storage across sessions. Version history only exists within the current conversation context. If the session is cleared or expires, all version history is permanently lost.

**4. Multi-module TC ID uniqueness (🟡 Major)**
If a spec has two modules with similar names (e.g., "User Login" and "Admin Login"), the auto-assigned module code may collide (both becoming `AUTH`), creating duplicate TC IDs in the report template.

**5. Retry mechanism scope (🟡 Major)**
Section 8 describes a 3-attempt retry with 5s delay and 30s timeout. Claude has no ability to retry its own API call or enforce a timeout from within the skill. This logic must exist in the calling application wrapper — if no wrapper exists (pure Claude Project usage), the retry mechanism never actually executes.

**6. Prompt injection resilience (🟡 Major)**
A malicious or poorly formatted spec could embed adversarial instructions attempting to hijack skill behavior or extract the system prompt. While Claude has built-in resistance, this is not guaranteed to hold for all injection patterns.

---

### Hidden Edge Cases Found

**Unicode and emoji in spec field names:** Field names with unicode characters (e.g., `用户名` for username) may cause TC ID generation and step descriptions to handle encoding inconsistently across platforms and browsers.

**Spec referencing deprecated endpoints:** If the spec says `POST /api/v1/auth/login` but the actual endpoint moved to `/api/v2/`, generated TCs will test the wrong endpoint with no warning from the skill.

**Pipe character `|` in spec content breaks Markdown tables:** If a spec field value contains an unescaped `|` (e.g., `Valid formats: JPG | PNG | WEBP`), it may break Markdown table rendering in the output.

**Spec contains image/diagram links with no alt text:** `![flowchart](url)` with no description is invisible to the skill, causing missed flow information without any warning to the user.

**Empty section headers in spec:** A spec with section headers but no content under them (e.g., `## Business Rules` immediately followed by `## Error Cases`) may cause skill to skip sections or generate TCs for the empty header text.

